<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n5708" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀμφιάνακτες</span></b> , <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ων</span>,
			οἱ, nickname of <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>dithyrambic poets,</i> because their odes often began
				thus—<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀμφί μοι αὖθις
			ἄνακτα</span> or <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀμφί μοι αὖτε,
				ἄναξ,</span> Sch.<i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Nu.</u>
				595
			</span></i>. <b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἀμφιανακτίζω</span></b> ,
				<i>sing dithyrambic hymns,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				Cratin.
				67
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Ar.
				<u>Fr.</u>
				59
			</span></i>, cf. foreg.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
